<!-- ID: executive_summary -->
Score: 84/100. Decision: BLOCK.

Package 2.2 stayed narrowly scoped and preserved the reported file boundary, warning codes, blocking semantics, and facade compatibility for the migrated scaffold/lifecycle families. All required pytest lanes passed and `git diff --check` was clean.

The package does not satisfy the full scope contract for protected scopes. `analyze_scaffold_quality` still emits `SCF_PLACEHOLDER_BRACKET` for placeholder literals inside inline code and indented code examples, so scaffold rules are not skipping protected scopes by default. That is a gate-blocking miss for Package 2.2 and makes Package 2.3 illegal to route.
<!-- ID: phase_review_results -->
## Phase Review Results

Package under review: Forge Package 2.2 — Scaffold And Lifecycle Rule Migration.

Pass checks:
- Scoped migration landed in `src/scribe_mcp/doc_management/quality/rules/scaffold.py` and facade delegation remains in `src/scribe_mcp/doc_management/scaffold_quality.py`.
- Existing scaffold warning codes and default blocking policies remain stable for the migrated families.
- Required regression additions for nested fenced content and markdown tables are present in `tests/test_manage_docs_scaffold_quality.py`.
- `CHECKLIST.md` proof for `p2-scaffold-rules` is readable, specific, and non-truncated.
- `pytest -q tests/test_manage_docs_scaffold_quality.py` -> `20 passed in 0.10s`
- `pytest -q tests/doc_management/test_quality_registry.py` -> `1 passed in 0.03s`
- `pytest -q tests/doc_management/test_quality_context.py` -> `2 passed in 0.07s`
- `pytest -q tests/test_manage_docs_quality_check.py` -> `11 passed in 18.22s`
- `git diff --check` -> clean

Blocking check:
- Protected-scope contract is not fully met. Direct probes still return `SCF_PLACEHOLDER_BRACKET` for inline-code and indented-code placeholder literals, so the rules are not skipping protected scopes by default.
<!-- ID: detailed_analysis -->
## Detailed Analysis

Why:
Package 2.2 is the first production migration of live scaffold-rule families onto the scoped registry path, so the gate question is whether the new evaluator preserved existing SCF behavior while actually honoring the new scope contract.

What:
I inspected `quality/rules/scaffold.py`, `scaffold_quality.py`, `tests/test_manage_docs_scaffold_quality.py`, and `CHECKLIST.md`, then compared the focused diff against the package contract. The migration stayed inside the expected files, delegated the scaffold/lifecycle/readiness/trailing-whitespace families through the new module, preserved the existing warning codes and default policy map, and did not migrate research-index or changelog families in this package.

How:
I verified source behavior and command evidence, then reproduced an uncovered protected-scope false positive with direct runtime probes:
- A `PYTHONPATH=src` probe calling `analyze_scaffold_quality` on an inline-code example literal still returns `SCF_PLACEHOLDER_BRACKET`.
- The same probe on a four-space-indented example line also returns `SCF_PLACEHOLDER_BRACKET`.

Those probes line up with the code shape. `evaluate_scaffold_rules()` passes `document_context` into the old helper layer, but `_in_code_fence()` only checks `context.offset_in_scope(..., kind="fenced_code")`, and `_placeholder_residue_warnings()` has no suppression for inline-code or indented-code protected examples. The tests added in this package cover nested fences and table rows, but they do not cover the broader protected-scope requirement stated in `PHASE_PLAN.md`.
<!-- ID: recommendations -->
## Recommendations

1. Extend the scoped suppression model so placeholder detection skips protected example scopes, not just fenced code.
2. Add regression coverage for inline-code and indented-code examples in `tests/test_manage_docs_scaffold_quality.py`.
3. Re-run the same four pytest lanes plus `git diff --check` after the fix and then resubmit Package 2.2 for package-specific validation.
4. Do not route Package 2.3 until this package has its own PASS.
